  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT has supported the recruitment and management of technical assistance to the Ministry of Education and Human Resources Development (MEHRD) to build capacity at national and provincial levels to support the drafting, passing and implementation of the new Solomon Islands Education Bill 2023. Australia&#x2019;s (and New Zealand&#x2019;s) support was central in enabling the Ministry to achieve this major reform. As a result of this investment 37 Provincial Education Providers are prepared to take on any additional responsibilities, including; Registration of Early Childhood Centres (ECE), Teacher Management and Establishment of the Education Coordination Office (PECO).MEHRD through support from the Education Sector Support Program (Australia -60% and New Zealand -40%) are working in close partnership with the different divisions (within the ministry) to ensure that they plan and implement changes systematically during the transition period.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Solomon Islands, the partner government through the Ministry of Education and Human Resources Development (MEHRD) completed the construction of two (2) girls&#x2019;, one (1) boys&#x2019; dormitory and ablution blocks projects and one (1) kitchen and dining hall.  Australia&#x2019;s total contribution was AUD1,434,052. In the reporting year, 794 people (687 female, 107 male) benefitted due to improved access to WASH facilities, secure accommodation and disability ramps. These infrastructure supports students and teacher health and well- being and access in overcoming barriers to access for Solomon Islands students.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
